SQL Normalization

Normalization is the process of organizing data in a database to reduce redundancy (duplicate data) and improve data integrity. It helps ensure that data is stored efficiently and consistently.

Why Normalize a Database?
• Eliminates duplicate data.
• Reduces storage space.
• Prevents data inconsistencies.
• Makes updates, inserts, and deletes easier.
• Improves overall database design.

Example (Unnormalized Table)

StudentID | StudentName | Course1 | Course2
------------------------------------------------
101       | Alice       | SQL     | Python
102       | Bob         | Java    | SQL

Problems:
• Multiple course columns create duplication.
• Difficult to add more courses.
• Data becomes harder to maintain.

Normalized Structure

Students Table

StudentID | StudentName
-----------------------
101       | Alice
102       | Bob

Courses Table

CourseID | CourseName
---------------------
1        | SQL
2        | Python
3        | Java

StudentCourses Table

StudentID | CourseID
--------------------
101       | 1
101       | 2
102       | 3
102       | 1

Benefits:
• Student information is stored once.
• Course information is stored once.
• Relationships are managed separately.
• Easier to maintain and scale.

Normal Forms

1NF (First Normal Form)
• Each column contains atomic (single) values.
• No repeating groups.

Bad:
ID | Phones
------------------------------
1  | 9876543210, 8765432109

Good:
ID | Phone
----------------
1  | 9876543210
1  | 8765432109

2NF (Second Normal Form)
• Must be in 1NF.
• Every non-key attribute must depend on the entire primary key.
• Eliminates partial dependencies.

3NF (Third Normal Form)
• Must be in 2NF.
• Non-key attributes should depend only on the primary key.
• Eliminates transitive dependencies.

Example:

Bad:
EmpID | DeptID | DeptName

Here, DeptName depends on DeptID, not directly on EmpID.

Good:
Employees(EmpID, DeptID)
Departments(DeptID, DeptName)

Advantages of Normalization
• Reduces data redundancy.
• Improves data consistency and integrity.
• Simplifies maintenance.
• Prevents update, insert, and delete anomalies.
• Makes database design more efficient.

Disadvantage
• Highly normalized databases often require more JOIN operations, which can make queries more complex.

Conclusion
Normalization is the process of dividing data into related tables and establishing relationships between them to minimize duplication, improve consistency, and maintain data integrity in SQL databases.
